1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,745
Scientists who want to know when humans

2
00:00:02,745 --> 00:00:05,169
started wearing clothes have found an

3
00:00:05,169 --> 00:00:06,502
unexpected witness:

4
00:00:06,502 --> 00:00:07,108
lice.

5
00:00:07,758 --> 00:00:09,367
Head lice live in your hair.

6
00:00:09,367 --> 00:00:10,925
Body lice live in clothing.

7
00:00:11,575 --> 00:00:14,678
And by comparing their DNA, researchers

8
00:00:14,678 --> 00:00:17,782
estimated that body lice split off from

9
00:00:17,782 --> 00:00:20,663
head lice roughly 170,000 years ago,

10
00:00:21,313 --> 00:00:23,608
which suggests that's around when our

11
00:00:23,608 --> 00:00:25,788
ancestors started regularly wearing

12
00:00:25,788 --> 00:00:26,419
clothes.

13
00:00:27,069 --> 00:00:28,270
That makes sense.

14
00:00:28,270 --> 00:00:30,732
Clothes kept us warm as we spread into

15
00:00:30,732 --> 00:00:31,692
colder lands.

16
00:00:32,342 --> 00:00:33,719
But here's the puzzle.

17
00:00:33,719 --> 00:00:36,143
Somewhere along the way, clothing stopped

18
00:00:36,143 --> 00:00:37,630
being only about warmth.

19
00:00:38,280 --> 00:00:40,940
Today, people pay thousands for a bag that

20
00:00:40,940 --> 00:00:43,305
holds the same things as a cheap one.

21
00:00:43,955 --> 00:00:45,316
They wear shoes that hurt.

22
00:00:45,966 --> 00:00:48,199
They throw away clothes that still work,

23
00:00:48,199 --> 00:00:49,966
because they're "out of style."

24
00:00:50,616 --> 00:00:52,209
Warmth explains clothing.

25
00:00:52,209 --> 00:00:54,030
It doesn't explain fashion.

26
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:56,075
So why did we invent it?

27
00:00:56,725 --> 00:00:59,037
If you like finding out why we do the

28
00:00:59,037 --> 00:01:01,176
strange things we do, subscribe.

29
00:01:01,176 --> 00:01:03,141
It genuinely helps the channel.

30
00:01:03,791 --> 00:01:05,567
The first clue is surprising:

31
00:01:05,567 --> 00:01:07,953
we may have decorated ourselves before

32
00:01:07,953 --> 00:01:09,340
we were fully dressed.

33
00:01:09,990 --> 00:01:12,838
In caves in Morocco, archaeologists found

34
00:01:12,838 --> 00:01:15,751
tiny sea shells with holes drilled through

35
00:01:15,751 --> 00:01:17,499
them, strung like beads.

36
00:01:18,149 --> 00:01:21,113
They're about 150,000 years old.

37
00:01:21,763 --> 00:01:24,971
In South Africa's Blombos Cave, similar

38
00:01:24,971 --> 00:01:28,256
shell beads are around 75,000 years old.

39
00:01:28,906 --> 00:01:30,405
Beads don't keep you warm.

40
00:01:30,405 --> 00:01:32,473
They don't protect you from anything.

41
00:01:33,123 --> 00:01:34,853
Their only job is to be seen.

42
00:01:35,503 --> 00:01:37,599
Archaeologists think they carried

43
00:01:37,599 --> 00:01:39,986
messages: who you are, which group you

44
00:01:39,986 --> 00:01:42,606
belong to, maybe whether you're married or

45
00:01:42,606 --> 00:01:43,362
important.

46
00:01:44,012 --> 00:01:46,354
In other words, long before writing,

47
00:01:46,354 --> 00:01:48,996
humans had already invented a way to wear

48
00:01:48,996 --> 00:01:50,858
information on their bodies.

49
00:01:51,508 --> 00:01:55,155
Jump forward to about 5,300 years ago.

50
00:01:55,805 --> 00:01:59,169
In 1991, hikers in the Alps found a frozen

51
00:01:59,169 --> 00:02:02,084
man in the ice. Scientists named him

52
00:02:02,084 --> 00:02:02,832
Ötzi.

53
00:02:03,482 --> 00:02:06,475
His clothes survived, and in 2016,

54
00:02:06,475 --> 00:02:09,225
researchers analyzed their DNA.

55
00:02:09,875 --> 00:02:12,145
His coat was made from sheep and goat

56
00:02:12,145 --> 00:02:14,585
leather, stitched in alternating strips.

57
00:02:15,235 --> 00:02:17,052
His hat was brown bear fur.

58
00:02:17,052 --> 00:02:18,628
His leggings were goat.

59
00:02:18,628 --> 00:02:20,143
His shoelaces, cattle.

60
00:02:20,793 --> 00:02:21,977
It was practical.

61
00:02:21,977 --> 00:02:23,636
But it was also chosen.

62
00:02:23,636 --> 00:02:25,946
The striped coat looks almost like a

63
00:02:25,946 --> 00:02:26,538
design.

64
00:02:27,188 --> 00:02:29,416
And Ötzi had more than sixty tattoos on

65
00:02:29,416 --> 00:02:30,053
his skin.

66
00:02:30,703 --> 00:02:33,086
Even a man walking alone through the

67
00:02:33,086 --> 00:02:35,774
mountains five thousand years ago was, in

68
00:02:35,774 --> 00:02:37,729
some way, dressed to be seen.

69
00:02:38,379 --> 00:02:40,832
Once clothing carried messages, it became

70
00:02:40,832 --> 00:02:42,002
a tool of power.

71
00:02:42,652 --> 00:02:44,936
In the ancient Mediterranean, the most

72
00:02:44,936 --> 00:02:47,220
prestigious color was Tyrian purple.

73
00:02:47,870 --> 00:02:50,133
It came from sea snails, and it took

74
00:02:50,133 --> 00:02:52,744
thousands of them to make a single gram of

75
00:02:52,744 --> 00:02:53,150
dye.

76
00:02:53,800 --> 00:02:55,796
Purple became the color of emperors.

77
00:02:56,446 --> 00:02:58,566
In Rome, wearing too much of it could get

78
00:02:58,566 --> 00:02:59,818
you in serious trouble.

79
00:03:00,468 --> 00:03:02,883
For centuries, many societies passed

80
00:03:02,883 --> 00:03:04,121
sumptuary laws:

81
00:03:04,121 --> 00:03:06,846
rules about who was allowed to wear what.

82
00:03:07,496 --> 00:03:10,087
In Elizabethan England, laws restricted

83
00:03:10,087 --> 00:03:12,554
certain silks, furs and colors to the

84
00:03:12,554 --> 00:03:13,294
nobility.

85
00:03:13,944 --> 00:03:16,451
In the Ottoman Empire, headgear and colors

86
00:03:16,451 --> 00:03:18,178
signaled rank and community.

87
00:03:18,828 --> 00:03:21,303
And when rulers wanted to change society,

88
00:03:21,303 --> 00:03:22,485
they changed hats.

89
00:03:23,135 --> 00:03:27,517
In 1829, Sultan Mahmud II introduced the

90
00:03:27,517 --> 00:03:29,757
fez for officials.

91
00:03:30,407 --> 00:03:33,214
Less than a century later, in 1925, the

92
00:03:33,214 --> 00:03:36,155
Turkish Republic's Hat Law replaced the

93
00:03:36,155 --> 00:03:38,027
fez with the brimmed hat.

94
00:03:38,677 --> 00:03:41,019
Clothing laws show something important:

95
00:03:41,019 --> 00:03:43,473
if governments bother to control what you

96
00:03:43,473 --> 00:03:45,424
wear, it's because clothes say

97
00:03:45,424 --> 00:03:46,149
something.

98
00:03:46,799 --> 00:03:49,494
Here's a fashion story that surprises most

99
00:03:49,494 --> 00:03:52,188
people: high heels were originally worn by

100
00:03:52,188 --> 00:03:52,727
men.

101
00:03:53,377 --> 00:03:55,897
Historians trace them to Persian horsemen,

102
00:03:55,897 --> 00:03:58,305
who used heeled boots to keep their feet

103
00:03:58,305 --> 00:03:59,761
steady in the stirrups.

104
00:04:00,411 --> 00:04:04,163
In the 1600s, European aristocrats adopted

105
00:04:04,163 --> 00:04:07,748
heels as a symbol of masculine, military

106
00:04:07,748 --> 00:04:08,498
style.

107
00:04:09,148 --> 00:04:11,456
France's Louis XIV loved them.

108
00:04:11,456 --> 00:04:14,226
His heels were often red, and red heels

109
00:04:14,226 --> 00:04:17,062
became a sign of belonging at his court.

110
00:04:17,712 --> 00:04:19,876
Then something interesting happened.

111
00:04:19,876 --> 00:04:22,208
As heels spread to ordinary people, the

112
00:04:22,208 --> 00:04:23,207
elite moved on,

113
00:04:23,857 --> 00:04:26,266
and over time heels became associated

114
00:04:26,266 --> 00:04:28,132
mainly with women's fashion.

115
00:04:28,782 --> 00:04:31,298
That pattern — the elite adopt something,

116
00:04:31,298 --> 00:04:33,700
others copy it, and the elite move on —

117
00:04:33,700 --> 00:04:35,987
turns out to be one of the engines of

118
00:04:35,987 --> 00:04:37,017
fashion itself.

119
00:04:37,667 --> 00:04:40,659
In 1904, the German sociologist Georg

120
00:04:40,659 --> 00:04:43,358
Simmel wrote a famous essay about

121
00:04:43,358 --> 00:04:44,161
fashion.

122
00:04:44,811 --> 00:04:47,253
He said fashion satisfies two opposite

123
00:04:47,253 --> 00:04:48,921
desires at the same time.

124
00:04:49,571 --> 00:04:51,242
The first is imitation:

125
00:04:51,242 --> 00:04:53,928
we want to fit in, to belong, to look like

126
00:04:53,928 --> 00:04:55,361
the people we admire.

127
00:04:56,011 --> 00:04:57,693
The second is distinction:

128
00:04:57,693 --> 00:05:00,134
we want to stand out, to be individual, to

129
00:05:00,134 --> 00:05:01,816
be different from the crowd.

130
00:05:02,466 --> 00:05:03,928
Fashion lets us do both.

131
00:05:04,578 --> 00:05:06,959
You follow a trend to belong to a group,

132
00:05:06,959 --> 00:05:09,451
and by following it, you separate yourself

133
00:05:09,451 --> 00:05:11,057
from everyone who doesn't.

134
00:05:11,707 --> 00:05:13,866
But this creates a never-ending cycle.

135
00:05:14,516 --> 00:05:17,028
Once everyone has copied a style, it can

136
00:05:17,028 --> 00:05:19,072
no longer make anyone stand out.

137
00:05:19,072 --> 00:05:21,292
So the people at the front move on.

138
00:05:21,942 --> 00:05:23,418
And the race begins again.

139
00:05:24,068 --> 00:05:26,126
That's why fashion has to change.

140
00:05:26,126 --> 00:05:28,456
A style that everyone wears stops saying

141
00:05:28,456 --> 00:05:29,106
anything.

142
00:05:29,756 --> 00:05:32,284
Clothes don't only send messages to other

143
00:05:32,284 --> 00:05:34,640
people. They may also send messages to

144
00:05:34,640 --> 00:05:35,099
us.

145
00:05:35,749 --> 00:05:38,773
In 2012, psychologists Hajo Adam and

146
00:05:38,773 --> 00:05:41,945
Adam Galinsky gave people a white coat

147
00:05:41,945 --> 00:05:42,756
to wear.

148
00:05:43,406 --> 00:05:45,592
Some were told it was a doctor's lab

149
00:05:45,592 --> 00:05:47,405
coat. Others were told it was a

150
00:05:47,405 --> 00:05:48,471
painter's coat.

151
00:05:48,471 --> 00:05:50,071
It was the exact same coat.

152
00:05:50,721 --> 00:05:52,980
Then everyone did a test that required

153
00:05:52,980 --> 00:05:54,137
careful attention.

154
00:05:54,787 --> 00:05:57,087
In their study, people who believed they

155
00:05:57,087 --> 00:05:59,332
were wearing a doctor's coat made fewer

156
00:05:59,332 --> 00:06:01,632
mistakes than those who thought it was a

157
00:06:01,632 --> 00:06:02,594
painter's coat.

158
00:06:03,244 --> 00:06:05,645
The researchers called this enclothed

159
00:06:05,645 --> 00:06:07,987
cognition: clothes can affect how we

160
00:06:07,987 --> 00:06:10,446
think, depending on what they mean to

161
00:06:10,446 --> 00:06:10,798
us.

162
00:06:11,448 --> 00:06:13,833
Later studies have had mixed results, so

163
00:06:13,833 --> 00:06:15,996
the effect may be smaller than first

164
00:06:15,996 --> 00:06:16,606
claimed.

165
00:06:17,256 --> 00:06:20,079
But the idea is familiar to anyone who has

166
00:06:20,079 --> 00:06:22,463
ever put on a uniform, a suit, or a

167
00:06:22,463 --> 00:06:25,224
favorite jacket, and felt like a slightly

168
00:06:25,224 --> 00:06:26,478
different person.

169
00:06:27,128 --> 00:06:29,378
There's another reason fashion is often

170
00:06:29,378 --> 00:06:31,146
expensive and impractical.

171
00:06:31,796 --> 00:06:35,369
In 1899, the economist Thorstein Veblen

172
00:06:35,369 --> 00:06:39,023
coined the term conspicuous consumption:

173
00:06:39,673 --> 00:06:41,812
spending money in ways that others can

174
00:06:41,812 --> 00:06:43,273
see, to show your status.

175
00:06:43,923 --> 00:06:46,724
He noticed that wealthy people often wore

176
00:06:46,724 --> 00:06:48,889
clothes that made physical work

177
00:06:48,889 --> 00:06:51,245
impossible: long sleeves, delicate

178
00:06:51,245 --> 00:06:52,964
fabrics, spotless white.

179
00:06:53,614 --> 00:06:54,890
The message was clear:

180
00:06:54,890 --> 00:06:56,930
I don't need to work with my hands.

181
00:06:57,580 --> 00:07:00,233
Biologists have a similar idea.

182
00:07:00,233 --> 00:07:03,276
In 1975, Amotz Zahavi proposed the

183
00:07:03,276 --> 00:07:05,148
handicap principle:

184
00:07:05,798 --> 00:07:08,544
a peacock's enormous tail is a burden, and

185
00:07:08,544 --> 00:07:10,863
that's exactly why it's impressive.

186
00:07:10,863 --> 00:07:13,059
Only a strong bird can afford it.

187
00:07:13,709 --> 00:07:15,950
A designer logo works the same way.

188
00:07:15,950 --> 00:07:18,426
Its message isn't "this is a good bag."

189
00:07:19,076 --> 00:07:21,295
Its message is "I can afford to waste

190
00:07:21,295 --> 00:07:22,349
money on a bag."

191
00:07:22,999 --> 00:07:25,964
And when everyone can buy logos, the very

192
00:07:25,964 --> 00:07:28,187
rich switch to "quiet luxury":

193
00:07:28,187 --> 00:07:31,219
no logos at all, just expensive simplicity

194
00:07:31,219 --> 00:07:33,374
that only insiders recognize.

195
00:07:34,024 --> 00:07:36,419
For most of history, the fashion cycle

196
00:07:36,419 --> 00:07:38,579
moved slowly, because clothes were

197
00:07:38,579 --> 00:07:40,681
expensive and took weeks to make.

198
00:07:41,331 --> 00:07:43,488
Today, the cycle runs at the speed of the

199
00:07:43,488 --> 00:07:44,076
internet.

200
00:07:44,726 --> 00:07:47,204
According to the Ellen MacArthur

201
00:07:47,204 --> 00:07:50,106
Foundation, global clothing production

202
00:07:50,106 --> 00:07:53,150
roughly doubled between 2000 and 2014,

203
00:07:53,800 --> 00:07:55,986
while the average number of times a

204
00:07:55,986 --> 00:07:58,517
garment was worn before being thrown away

205
00:07:58,517 --> 00:08:00,071
fell by about a third.

206
00:08:00,721 --> 00:08:03,367
The ancient desire to signal "I belong,

207
00:08:03,367 --> 00:08:06,076
but I'm also unique" now plays out every

208
00:08:06,076 --> 00:08:07,588
week, on every phone.

209
00:08:08,238 --> 00:08:10,915
The price is paid by the planet, and often

210
00:08:10,915 --> 00:08:13,235
by the workers who make the clothes.

211
00:08:13,885 --> 00:08:17,465
In 2013, the Rana Plaza factory building

212
00:08:17,465 --> 00:08:21,045
in Bangladesh collapsed, killing more than

213
00:08:21,045 --> 00:08:23,034
1,100 garment workers.

214
00:08:23,684 --> 00:08:26,014
Many of them had been making clothes for

215
00:08:26,014 --> 00:08:27,585
well-known Western brands.

216
00:08:28,235 --> 00:08:30,397
Try this. Look at what you're wearing

217
00:08:30,397 --> 00:08:31,100
right now.

218
00:08:31,750 --> 00:08:33,486
Ask yourself three questions.

219
00:08:33,486 --> 00:08:35,602
What does it say about which group I

220
00:08:35,602 --> 00:08:36,307
belong to?

221
00:08:36,958 --> 00:08:38,831
What does it say about how I'm different?

222
00:08:39,481 --> 00:08:41,586
And who, exactly, am I dressing for?

223
00:08:42,236 --> 00:08:44,498
Even if you think you "don't care about

224
00:08:44,498 --> 00:08:46,329
fashion," that's a message too.

225
00:08:46,979 --> 00:08:48,940
Choosing not to play is still a move in

226
00:08:48,940 --> 00:08:49,500
the game.

227
00:08:50,150 --> 00:08:52,555
We invented clothing to survive the cold.

228
00:08:53,205 --> 00:08:55,058
But we invented fashion to talk:

229
00:08:55,708 --> 00:08:58,195
to say who we are, who we're with, and who

230
00:08:58,195 --> 00:09:00,572
we're not, without saying a single word.

231
00:09:01,222 --> 00:09:03,496
A hundred and fifty thousand years after

232
00:09:03,496 --> 00:09:05,769
the first shell beads, we're still doing

233
00:09:05,769 --> 00:09:06,086
it.

234
00:09:06,736 --> 00:09:08,882
Every morning, in front of the mirror, we

235
00:09:08,882 --> 00:09:09,955
choose what to say.

